{"name":"Easy Breezy","hex":"#aacdd8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"easy-breezy","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Laid-back and uncomplicated."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/easy-breezy"}],"entries":[{"title":"Easy Breezy","blocks":[{"text":"\"Easy Breezy\" is Utada's debut English single. It is also the first single from Exodus, in which it also appears. For this release, they are using Island Def Jam as their label, under the name of simply Utada, rather than Hikaru Utada which was used for their Japanese releases. This was originally released as an exclusive download single, though was later released as a DVD single in Japan. This song has been used in several Nintendo DS endorsements featuring Utada. There was a promo single for Easy Breezy that was sent to radio stations that features an exclusive radio edit of the song. The cover is the same as for the DVD single. The single sold 2,731 copies in Korea."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/d8/UTADA-Easy_Breezy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Easy_Breezy","wikidata":"Q3717807"},{"title":"Breezy","blocks":[{"text":"Breezy is a 1973 American romantic drama film directed by Clint Eastwood, produced by Robert Daley, and written by Jo Heims. The film stars William Holden and Kay Lenz, with Roger C. Carmel, Marj Dusay, and Joan Hotchkis in supporting roles. It is the third film directed by Eastwood and the first without him starring in it."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/a7/Breezyfilmposter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breezy","wikidata":"Q653251"}],"images":[],"sounds":{},"pictures":[{"title":"Grey heron (00129p)","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Grey_heron_%2800129p%29.jpg/500px-Grey_heron_%2800129p%29.jp